I would strongly argue against this.  I think it would be confusing and lead to a lot of problems.  For instance, with the above, x[1,:,:] would be a 4x5 matrix but what would x[:,:,1] be?  If it's a 3x4 matrix, then it would involve a lot of unpleasant copying.  So long as internally a matrix has to be 2D, that should be explicit to users, like it is now.

In a world with more hours in the day I would go and implement a multidimensional array with a Map for Eigen::Matrix to allow us to just do:

real x[3,4];

instead of

matrix[3,4] x;

or

real[0,1] x[7,8];

and be done with it :)

At any rate, since this is all started based on a feature that's not yet implemented (vectors and matrices of bounded variables) and is not likely to be widely used at first I think we can let it rest for the near term.  There are options which are consistent with the current notation (matrix<real[0,1]>[3,4] for instance) and don't cause parsing problems.  And, since it relates to the larger issue of multidimensional arrays, it may get solved in another way at some point in the future.

Cheers,
Marcus

