Have we validated the rejections are occurring around tau = 0 and not some other pathological region of the parameter space?

On Aug 12, 2012, at 3:16 PM, Andrew Gelman wrote:

> My guess, based on my paper with Van Dyk et al., is that no step size will work if the sims happen to get close enough to 0.  The only way I can see right now to solve it are:  (a) parameter expansion, or (b) a prior that is zero at 0 and increases linearly from there in some small local region near 0.  This should have the effect of kicking the sampler away from 0, which is what we want.
>
> On the upside, we're no worse off than Bugs etc.
>
> Another approach would be to have a possibility that is tied to diagnostics, where if there is a place that happens to be close to the boundary (e.g., tau near 0) where nothing's accepting, that we give the user the automatic option of slapping down a prior that kicks things away from 0.  Given that these problems shoudl generally be happening at boundaries, this might actually be a reasonable option!
>
> A
>
>
> On Aug 12, 2012, at 7:47 PM, Bob Carpenter wrote:
>
>> What I should've been asking is how small the
>> step size needs to be and then how many moves on
>> that small scale you need.  If it's very small,
>> uniformly sampling on an interval won't get there.
>> But inserting known small steps every few iterations
>> could.  And it would be very easy to implement.
>>
>> - Bob
>>
>> On 8/11/12 7:21 PM, Michael Betancourt wrote:
>>> It seems like this is the case that motivates not just a random jitter but also some large "jerks" towards smaller step sizes, a la Neal.
>>>
>>> The graphical diagnoses should also pick this up.
>>>
>>> On Aug 11, 2012, at 5:21 PM, Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com> wrote:
>>>
>>>> How easy is it to get out of the bad spot with
>>>> a small enough step size?  We can randomize step
>>>> sizes around the one found by adaptation.
>>>>
>>>> I think we will be able to do some higher-level
>>>> modeling.  It should be easy to add to the Stan
>>>> language.
>>>>
>>>> - Bob
>>>>
>>>>
>>>> On 8/11/12 4:16 PM, Andrew Gelman wrote:
>>>>> The 8 schools (actually, any hierarchical model) actually will take forever in Metropolis or Gibbs if you get close
>>>>> enough to the tau=0 point.  This is discussed in my JCGS paper with David Van Dyk.
>>>>>
>>>>> One way to solve the problem (in Gibbs/Metropolis, possibly also in HMC) is to do redundant parameterization.  I
>>>>> actually demonstrate that in the 8 schools case in Appendix C of BDA.  It is a mild annoyance, though.
>>>>>
>>>>> I am expecting that one of our upcoming resarch projects (once stan is fully running) is to set up a new notation and
>>>>> coding for hierarchical models, in which case perhaps the redundant parameterization can be done invisibly.  Also
>>>>> there's the issue of saving finite-sample comparisons as quantities of intersest.  We can discuss further when we have
>>>>> time to think about this.
>>>>>
>>>>> For now, I think we can accept that we have a problem that Bugs etc also have.
>>>>>
>>>>> See you
>>>>> A
>>>>>
>>>>> On Aug 11, 2012, at 9:35 PM, Matt Hoffman wrote:
>>>>>
>>>>>> The problem is that it hits a region that's sufficiently unfriendly that it can't take even one step successfully. Hmc
>>>>>> should also have this problem.
>>>>>>
>>>>>> Arguably this means that the stepsize adaptation is failing to find a step size that's small enough for the whole
>>>>>> parameter space. Which isn't surprising given that it takes so long to find these difficult spots.
>>>>>>
>>>>>> On Friday, August 10, 2012, Andrew Gelman wrote:
>>>>>>
>>>>>>   I thought that shouldn't happen with Nuts becasue then it would just take really short trajcetories?
>>>>>>
>>>>>>   On Aug 11, 2012, at 1:47 AM, Matt Hoffman wrote:
>>>>>>
>>>>>>> getting to a place where you wind up rejecting for a long time and
>>>>>>> screwing up your ESS. This isn't because of bad adaptation—it happens
>>>>>>> regardless of how long you adapt for.
>>>>>>
>>>>>
