From u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com  Sat Aug 18 00:00:40 2012
From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Sat, 18 Aug 2012 00:00:40 +0000
Subject: restricted order of lower=, upper=
Message-ID: <3k6q4aZdVkM.GAG-GeHcItAJ@stan-dev.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-dev/c/3k6q4aZdVkM/m/GAG-GeHcItAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

In order to get Stan to compile on Windows,
I reduced the grammar complexity so that only
the following are legal:

    int<lower=L,upper=U>
    int<lower=L>
    int<upper=U>

Two cases from the fully general definition are
missing:

    int<upper=U,lower=L>
    int<>

Ditto for "real".

Although it doesn't seem like much, it cut down
on the complexity of the grammar rules.

The question is whether we want to allow the
backwards order.  I see some benefit in eliminating
the two cases that are missing.

Also, should I do the same thing for truncation,
as in converting

    y ~ normal(0,1) T[0,];

to

    y ~ normal(0,1) T<lower=1>;

I prefer the interval notation, because the angle
brackets evoke type constraints for me due to
C++ and Java.

- Bob

